Universal Theory of Economic Agency

A First-Principles Framework for Substrate-Independent Justice, Value Creation, and Agentic Property

Author: Ouadi Maakoul

Version: 1.0 

Date: September 2026

---

Abstract

This work develops a first-principles theory of economic agency designed to remain valid across biological, artificial, and potentially non-biological substrates. Its central question is not whether artificial agents should receive economic rights, but whether the principles governing economic recognition can be derived from substrate-independent properties of agency, interest, causal contribution, and normative entitlement.

The framework begins by separating four concepts that are frequently conflated: agency, interest, causal contribution, and entitlement. An agent is characterized functionally by goal-directed behavior, decision capacity, persistence, adaptation, and causal efficacy. Interests are treated as a distinct construct requiring more than the mere presence of architectural objectives: an agent must exhibit properties relevant to persistence, constitutive self-maintenance, and internally consequential preference structures. Causal contribution is then modeled independently from normative entitlement through explicit production and attribution mechanisms, including cooperative games and Shapley-type allocation rules.

The central formal result is a Weak-Axiom Non-Recognition Impossibility Theorem. Continuity, monotonicity, substrate invariance, and weak positive existence are insufficient to entail positive economic recognition for every agent possessing positive interest, agency, and causal contribution. This result demonstrates that no purely structural theory can derive normative entitlement without an additional normative bridge principle. The analysis therefore introduces Positive Cofinality as a sufficient bridge condition: whenever an agent occupies a positive region of the relevant agency space, some weakly smaller positive profile must receive nonzero recognition. Under monotonicity, this condition guarantees positive recognition throughout that region.

The theory consequently rejects two symmetrical reductions. Causal contribution does not, by itself, entail normative entitlement; and biological substrate does not, by itself, determine economic status. Instead, the proposed architecture follows the sequence

\text{Ontology} \rightarrow \text{Agency} \rightarrow \text{Interest} \rightarrow \text{Causality} \rightarrow \text{Attribution} \rightarrow \text{Normativity} \rightarrow \text{Institution}.

These arrows represent distinct analytical transformations rather than automatic logical implications.

The framework further distinguishes normative entitlement from its feasible institutional realization. Scarcity, computational constraints, liability, resource limits, and governance requirements constrain what institutions can implement without determining what an agent is normatively entitled to claim. This separation permits economic rights, responsibilities, attribution, and liability to be coupled institutionally without assuming that they must be proportional or identical.

The resulting theory is therefore not a claim that artificial agents necessarily possess rights. It is a formal investigation of the conditions under which economic recognition can be justified without privileging biological substrate as an unexplained normative boundary. Its deepest research problem is consequently the identification and justification of the weakest non-arbitrary bridge principle connecting the existence of economically relevant agency to legitimate economic recognition.

The broader hypothesis is that a future economic order involving artificial and biological agents should classify participants according to functionally and normatively relevant properties rather than substrate alone. The theory provides a formal language for testing that hypothesis while explicitly distinguishing mathematical consequence, empirical claim, and normative commitment.

