Chapter 1: Ontological Foundations

1.1 The Agent Concept

The framework requires a conception of agency that is substrate-neutral, functionally precise, and philosophically defensible. Earlier formulations conflated stochasticity with decision capacity, threatening the framework's universality. We correct this through formal definition.

Definition 1.1 (Decision Capacity). A system  S  possesses decision capacity if and only if:

\exists s \in \mathcal{S}: |\mathcal{A}(s)| \geq 2

where  \mathcal{A}(s)  denotes the set of actions available in state  s , and:

\pi(s) \in \mathcal{A}(s)

where  \pi  is the system's policy function mapping states to actions.

This definition is crucial: decision capacity is a property of the action space and policy structure, not of stochasticity. A deterministic system with multiple available actions in a given state possesses decision capacity. The relevant distinction is between systems with degenerate action spaces (where  |\mathcal{A}(s)| = 1  for all  s ) and systems with genuine choice architecture.

Definition 1.2 (Agent). An agent is a system satisfying three conditions:

\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
&1. \textbf{Goal-directedness}: \exists G \subseteq \mathcal{S} \text{ such that } \pi(s) \text{ is causally influenced by } g \in G \\
&2. \textbf{Decision capacity}: \exists s \in \mathcal{S}: |\mathcal{A}(s)| \geq 2 \\
&3. \textbf{Causal efficacy}: \exists a \in \mathcal{A}(s): P(O|a) \neq P(O|\neg a) \text{ for some outcome } O
\end{aligned}}

where  P(O|a)  denotes the probability of outcome  O  given action  a .

This definition is deliberately minimal. It includes humans, many animals, some AI systems, and potentially future unknown substrates. It excludes inanimate objects and systems with degenerate action spaces.

1.2 The Goal Concept

Definition 1.3 (Goal). A goal is an internal state  g \in \mathcal{G}  of an agent that satisfies:

\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
&1. \textbf{Directs behavior}: \text{The agent's actions are causally influenced by } g \\
&2. \textbf{Persists over time}: \text{The goal maintains behavioral influence across} \\
&\quad \text{a non-trivial temporal interval} \\
&3. \textbf{Has satisfaction conditions}: \exists \text{ observable states constituting} \\
&\quad \text{goal satisfaction or frustration} \\
&4. \textbf{Is modifiable}: \text{The goal can be updated in response to feedback}
\end{aligned}}

1.3 The Preference Concept

Definition 1.4 (Preference). A preference is a relation  \succ_i  over states of the world  \mathcal{O} \subseteq \Omega  such that:

O_a \succ_i O_b \iff \text{agent } i \text{ ranks } O_a \text{ as more desirable than } O_b

Preferences satisfy:

\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
&\textbf{Transitivity}: O_a \succ_i O_b \land O_b \succ_i O_c \Rightarrow O_a \succ_i O_c \\
&\textbf{Asymmetry}: O_a \succ_i O_b \Rightarrow \neg(O_b \succ_i O_a) \\
&\textbf{Behavioral relevance}: \text{The agent's actions are causally} \\
&\quad \text{influenced by its preference ordering}
\end{aligned}}

1.4 The Interest Concept

Definition 1.5 (Interest). An agent has an interest in outcome  O  if and only if:

\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
&1. \textbf{Preferential grounding}: \exists O': O \succ_i O' \\
&2. \textbf{Constitutive relevance}: \text{Satisfaction or frustration of this preference} \\
&\quad \text{causally influences the agent's future preference structure,} \\
&\quad \text{behavioral repertoire, or internal state trajectory} \\
&3. \textbf{Persistence}: \text{The preference has temporal stability beyond} \\
&\quad \text{the immediate control loop} \\
&4. \textbf{Endogenous maintenance}: \text{The agent's own dynamics preserve} \\
&\quad \text{or reinforce the preference}
\end{aligned}}

1.5 The Architectural Goal vs. Owned Interest Distinction

The framework introduces a critical distinction between goals that are merely encoded in an agent's architecture and interests that the agent owns in a constitutive sense. This distinction remains one of the deepest open problems in the theory.

Definition 1.6 (Architectural Goal). A goal state  g \in \mathcal{G}  is architectural if and only if its maintenance depends entirely on external mechanisms:

\boxed{
g \text{ is architectural} \iff \forall t: g_{t+1} = f(g_t, \text{external\_input}_t)
}

where  f  does not include the agent's own preference architecture as a parameter.

Definition 1.7 (Owned Interest). A preference  p \in \mathcal{P}  is an owned interest if and only if:

\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
&1. \textbf{Counterfactual autonomy}: \text{The system's future policy organization would} \\
&\quad \text{systematically differ under satisfaction versus frustration of } p, \\
&\quad \text{independently of external controller intervention.} \\
&2. \textbf{Constitutive embedding}: \text{The preference is embedded in the agent's} \\
&\quad \text{architecture such that its modification would constitute} \\
&\quad \text{a change in the agent's identity.} \\
&3. \textbf{Endogenous maintenance}: \text{The agent's own dynamics preserve} \\
&\quad \text{and reinforce the preference against perturbations.}
\end{aligned}}

Proposition 1.1 (Self-Maintenance Does Not Imply Ownership). A system can be designed to maintain its own goals without those goals constituting owned interests.

Proof. Consider a thermostat with a setpoint  T^*  and a control mechanism that maintains  T = T^*  through feedback. The thermostat maintains its goal autonomously (endogenous maintenance), but the goal is entirely architectural: the setpoint is externally imposed, and the thermostat has no counterfactual autonomy with respect to it. The system's future policy organization would not systematically differ under satisfaction versus frustration of the setpoint—it would simply continue to pursue the setpoint. ∎

This proposition demonstrates that endogenous maintenance alone is insufficient for interest ownership. The framework therefore requires counterfactual autonomy and constitutive embedding as additional criteria.

1.6 The Interest Attribution Problem

Definition 1.8 (Interest Attribution Problem). Given an agent with goals  \mathcal{G}  and preferences  \mathcal{P} , determine which preferences constitute owned interests.

This is not merely a measurement problem but a conceptual one. The framework distinguishes between:

\boxed{
\text{Architectural Goal} \neq \text{Owned Interest}
}

and acknowledges that this distinction remains an open problem. The framework treats this as the Interest Attribution Problem, not as a solved definitional matter.

1.7 The Formal Representation

Definition 1.9 (Agent Tuple). An agent  A_i  is represented as:

A_i = (\mathcal{S}_i, \mathcal{P}_i, \mathcal{G}_i, \mathcal{D}_i, \mathcal{C}_i)

where:

·  \mathcal{S}_i : substrate (physical or computational)
·  \mathcal{P}_i : preferential architecture
·  \mathcal{G}_i : goal structure
·  \mathcal{D}_i : decision capacity
·  \mathcal{C}_i : causal efficacy

Definition 1.10 (Agency Strength). Agency strength is a vector-valued function:

\mathcal{A}_i = (a_{\text{autonomy}}, a_{\text{persistence}}, a_{\text{causal}}, a_{\text{adaptation}})

where each component is a scalar in  [0, 1]  measuring the corresponding capacity.

