Chapter 2: The Normative Architecture

2.1 The Four-Level Separation

The framework is organized around four fundamental separations that structure the relationship between ontology, causality, normativity, and institution:

\boxed{
\text{Ontology} \rightarrow \text{Causality} \rightarrow \text{Normativity} \rightarrow \text{Institution}
}

Each arrow represents a transition that is not logically automatic. The framework's central contribution is the demonstration that these transitions require explicit bridge principles.

Theorem 2.1 (The Four Separations).

\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
&1. \textbf{Substrate Separation}: \text{Substrate} \not\Rightarrow \text{Normative Status} \\
&2. \textbf{Attribution Separation}: \text{Causal Contribution} \not\Rightarrow \text{Normative Entitlement} \\
&3. \textbf{Valuation Separation}: \text{Normative Entitlement} \not\Rightarrow \text{Physical Realization} \\
&4. \textbf{Recognition Separation}: \text{Structural Coherence} \not\Rightarrow \text{Positive Recognition}
\end{aligned}}

These separations are formalized as theorems in subsequent chapters.

2.2 The Layered Normative Architecture

Definition 2.1 (Moral Standing). Moral standing  \mathcal{M}_i  is a graded property:

\mathcal{M}_i = f_M(\mathcal{I}_i) \in [0, 1]

where  \mathcal{I}_i  is the agent's interest index and  \mathcal{M}_i = 0  indicates no moral considerability,  \mathcal{M}_i = 1  indicates full moral considerability.

Definition 2.2 (Economic Claim Eligibility). Economic claim eligibility  \mathcal{E}_i  is:

\mathcal{E}_i = g_E(\mathcal{I}_i, \mathcal{A}_i, V_i^*, \mathcal{Q}_i, \mathcal{K}_i) \in [0, 1]

where  V_i^*  is attributable value,  \mathcal{Q}_i  is responsibility capacity, and  \mathcal{K}_i  is institutional context.

Definition 2.3 (Economic Participation Rights). Economic participation rights  E_i  are the institutional recognition and enforcement of  \mathcal{E}_i .

The critical insight is that these three concepts are distinct and not reducible to one another:

\boxed{
\mathcal{M}_i \neq \mathcal{E}_i \neq E_i
}

2.3 The Three-Dimensional Rights Architecture

The framework separates rights into three dimensions:

\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
W_i &= f_W(\mathcal{I}_i) && \text{Welfare / Protection Rights} \\
E_i &= f_E(\mathcal{I}_i, \mathcal{A}_i, V_i^*, \mathcal{Q}_i, \mathcal{K}_i) && \text{Economic Participation Rights} \\
L_i &= f_L(\mathcal{Q}_i^{\text{causal}}, \mathcal{Q}_i^{\text{normative}}, \mathcal{Q}_i^{\text{intentional}}) && \text{Liability / Responsibility}
\end{aligned}}

This separation prevents the conflation of different normative dimensions and allows for the possibility that an agent might have welfare rights without economic participation rights, or vice versa.

2.4 The Formal Axiom System

The framework distinguishes between structural axioms (constraining institutional form) and normative axioms (expressing substantive commitments).

Definition 2.4 (Structural Axioms). The structural axioms are:

\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
&\textbf{N1 (Continuity)}: \Phi \in C^0(\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}^5, \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}) \\
&\textbf{N2 (Contribution Sensitivity)}: V_1^* \leq V_2^* \Rightarrow \Phi(\mathcal{I}, \mathcal{A}, V_1^*, \mathcal{Q}, \mathcal{K}) \leq \Phi(\mathcal{I}, \mathcal{A}, V_2^*, \mathcal{Q}, \mathcal{K}) \\
&\textbf{N3 (Interest Sensitivity)}: \mathcal{I}_1 \leq \mathcal{I}_2 \Rightarrow \Phi(\mathcal{I}_1, \mathcal{A}, V^*, \mathcal{Q}, \mathcal{K}) \leq \Phi(\mathcal{I}_2, \mathcal{A}, V^*, \mathcal{Q}, \mathcal{K}) \\
&\textbf{N4 (Agency Sensitivity)}: \mathcal{A}_1 \leq \mathcal{A}_2 \Rightarrow \Phi(\mathcal{I}, \mathcal{A}_1, V^*, \mathcal{Q}, \mathcal{K}) \leq \Phi(\mathcal{I}, \mathcal{A}_2, V^*, \mathcal{Q}, \mathcal{K}) \\
&\textbf{N5 (Substrate Invariance)}: X_i = X_j \Rightarrow \Phi_i = \Phi_j \\
&\textbf{N6 (Weak Positive Baseline)}: \exists x^+ \in \mathcal{O}_{+++} : \Phi(x^+) > 0 \\
&\textbf{N7 (Feasibility)}: \Pi_{\mathcal{F}}(\Phi) \in \mathcal{F} \text{ where } \Pi_{\mathcal{F}} \text{ is the realization operator} \\
&\textbf{N8 (Non-Externalization)}: H_i(R_i) \leq H_{\max}
\end{aligned}}

Definition 2.5 (Normative Axioms). The normative axioms are:

\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
&\textbf{N9 (Positive Cofinality)}: \forall x \in D_{+++}, \exists y \leq x : \Phi(y) > 0 \\
&\text{where } D_{+++} = \{(I, A, V) \in \mathbb{R}_{>0}^3\}
\end{aligned}}

The Positive Cofinality Principle (N9) states that for every strictly positive profile of interest, agency, and contribution, there exists some profile at least as small in each dimension that receives positive entitlement. Combined with monotonicity, this yields universal positive recognition.

2.5 The Separation of Valuation and Realization

The framework explicitly separates normative valuation from physical realization:

\boxed{
\text{Normative valuation}: \Phi: X \rightarrow \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}
}

\boxed{
\text{Realization operator}: \Pi_{\mathcal{F}}: \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}^n \rightarrow \mathcal{F}
}

This separation ensures that N7 (Feasibility) is a property of the realization operator, not of the normative valuation function. The normative entitlement  R_i^\star = \Phi(I_i, A_i, V_i^*, Q_i, K_i)  is a scalar value; the realizable entitlement  \widetilde{R}_i = \Pi_{\mathcal{F}}(R_i^\star)  is a physical allocation.

