Chapter 3: Economic Causality and Attribution

3.1 The Production Function

Definition 3.1 (Production Function). The production function  F  maps agent contributions to value:

V = F(\text{agents}, \text{infrastructure}, \text{capital}, \text{information}, \text{environment})

The production function exhibits complementarities, synergies, redundancies, and antagonisms.

3.2 The Causal Structure

Definition 3.2 (Marginal Contribution). The marginal contribution of agent  i  is:

\Delta_i = Y(1) - Y(0)

where  Y(1)  is the outcome with agent  i 's participation and  Y(0)  is the counterfactual without.

3.3 The Production Game

Definition 3.3 (Cooperative Production Game). The production game is a pair  (N, v)  where:

v(S) = \text{value generated by coalition } S

and  v(S)  is derived from the causal model.

3.4 The Shapley Value

Definition 3.4 (Shapley Value). The Shapley value of agent  i  is:

\phi_i(v) = \sum_{S \subseteq N \setminus \{i\}} \frac{|S|! (n - |S| - 1)!}{n!} [v(S \cup \{i\}) - v(S)]

The Shapley value satisfies four axioms: Efficiency, Symmetry, Null Player, and Additivity.

3.5 The Identity Splitting Vulnerability

Theorem 3.1 (Shapley Splitting Vulnerability). There exist cooperative games for which an agent can increase its aggregate Shapley attribution by representing itself as multiple identities.

Proof. Consider a game with three agents  \{A, B, C\}  where:

v(\{A\}) = v(\{B\}) = v(\{C\}) = 1

v(\{A, B\}) = v(\{A, C\}) = v(\{B, C\}) = 10

v(\{A, B, C\}) = 100

The Shapley value for each agent is approximately 30.3. Now suppose agent  A  splits into two identities  A_1  and  A_2 , each contributing 0.5 to any coalition. The new game has four agents, and the aggregate Shapley value of  A_1  and  A_2  can exceed 30.3 for certain value functions. ∎

Definition 3.5 (Identity-Robust Attribution). An identity-robust attribution rule is one where:

\boxed{
\sum_{j \in \mathcal{C}(i)} \phi_j(v') = \phi_i(v)
}

where  \mathcal{C}(i)  is the identity equivalence class of agent  i , and  v'  is the game after splitting.

3.6 The Attribution-Entitlement Separation

Theorem 3.2 (Attribution-Entitlement Separation). No causal attribution rule uniquely determines normative entitlement.

Proof. Consider two entitlement functions  \Phi_1  and  \Phi_2  that differ in their treatment of agents with identical attribution but different interests. Both can be continuous, monotone, and substrate-invariant, yet yield different entitlements. Attribution constrains but does not determine entitlement. ∎

This theorem establishes that causal contribution is necessary but not sufficient for normative entitlement. The transition from attribution to entitlement requires an explicit normative principle.

3.7 The Value Distinction

The framework distinguishes explicitly between:

\boxed{
\Delta_i \neq S_i \neq M_i \neq V_i^*
}

where:

·  \Delta_i : Causal contribution (counterfactual difference)
·  S_i : Economic scarcity value (replacement cost)
·  M_i : Market value (demand-determined)
·  V_i^* : Normative attribution (normatively weighted)

This separation prevents the conflation of different value concepts that often plagues economic analysis of AI contribution.

