Chapter 4: The Entitlement Theory

4.1 The Entitlement Function

Definition 4.1 (Normative Entitlement). The normative entitlement of agent  i  is:

R_i^\star = \Phi(\mathcal{I}_i, \mathcal{A}_i, V_i^*, \mathcal{Q}_i, \mathcal{K}_i)

where  \Phi  is the entitlement function satisfying the structural and normative axioms.

4.2 The Candidate Entitlement Function

Definition 4.2 (Saturating Entitlement Function). A candidate entitlement function is:

\Phi = \kappa(\mathcal{K}) \cdot \frac{(1 + \mathcal{I}^{\alpha})(1 + \mathcal{A}^{\beta})(1 + (V^*)^{\gamma})(1 + \mathcal{Q}^{\delta}) - 1}{C + (1 + \mathcal{I}^{\alpha})(1 + \mathcal{A}^{\beta})(1 + (V^*)^{\gamma})(1 + \mathcal{Q}^{\delta}) - 1}

where:

·  \alpha, \beta, \gamma, \delta > 0 : elasticity parameters
·  \kappa(\mathcal{K}) > 0 : institutional scaling factor
·  C > 0 : saturation constant

This function satisfies the structural axioms (N1–N6) and the Positive Cofinality Principle (N9). It represents one member of a broader class of admissible entitlement functions.

Proposition 4.1 (Continuity of the Saturating Function). The saturating entitlement function is continuous in all arguments.

Proof. The function is a quotient of continuous functions, with denominator strictly positive for all inputs (since  C > 0  and the numerator is  \geq 0 ). Therefore  \Phi  is continuous. ∎

4.3 The Weak-Axiom Impossibility Theorem

Theorem 4.2 (Weak-Axiom Impossibility). Let  \Phi: \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}^5 \rightarrow \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}  satisfy Axioms N1 (Continuity), N2–N4 (monotone non-decreasing in  I, A, V^* ), N5 (Substrate Invariance), and N6 (Weak Positive Baseline). Then these axioms do not imply:

\forall (I, A, V^*, Q, K) \in \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}^5 \text{ with } I > 0, A > 0, V^* > 0: \Phi(I, A, V^*, Q, K) > 0

Proof. Consider the counterexample:

\Phi(I, A, V^*, Q, K) = \max\{0, I + A + V^* - C\}, \qquad C > 0

This function satisfies:

· N1:  \max\{0, \cdot\}  and linear combination are continuous
· N2–N4: Monotone non-decreasing in  I, A, V^* 
· N5: Depends only on functional profile
· N6: For  I + A + V^* > C ,  \Phi > 0 

Yet for any profile with  0 < I + A + V^* \leq C :

\Phi(I, A, V^*, Q, K) = 0

Therefore, there exist strictly positive profiles with zero entitlement. ∎

This theorem demonstrates that structural axioms alone do not generate universal positive economic recognition. The genuinely normative content must enter through explicit principles.

4.4 The Positive Cofinality Theorem

Theorem 4.3 (Positive Cofinality Theorem). Let  \Phi  satisfy Axioms N1–N5 and the Positive Cofinality Principle (N9). Then:

\forall (I, A, V^*, Q, K) \in \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}^5 \text{ with } I > 0, A > 0, V^* > 0: \Phi(I, A, V^*, Q, K) > 0

Proof. Let  x = (I, A, V^*, Q, K)  be any profile with  I > 0, A > 0, V^* > 0 .

By Positive Cofinality (N9), there exists  y = (I', A', V', Q, K) \leq (I, A, V^*)  such that  \Phi(y) > 0 .

By Axioms N2–N4 (monotonicity in  I, A, V^* ), since  y \leq x  componentwise in these dimensions:

\Phi(x) \geq \Phi(y) > 0

Therefore  \Phi(x) > 0 . ∎

This theorem is mathematically straightforward but philosophically significant. It demonstrates that the Positive Cofinality Principle, combined with monotonicity, is sufficient to generate universal positive recognition. The bridge between structural coherence and positive recognition is explicit and minimal.

4.5 The Substrate Invariance Theorem

Theorem 4.4 (Substrate Invariance). If agents  i  and  j  have identical functional profiles  (I, A, V^*, Q, K) , then any admissible entitlement function assigns them identical entitlements.

Proof. This is Axiom N5. ∎

This theorem establishes that substrate is not a sufficient basis for differential treatment when functional profiles are identical. It rules out arbitrary discrimination based on physical substrate while leaving open the possibility of differential treatment based on functional differences.

