Chapter 6: Institutional Stability

6.1 The Measurement Problem

Definition 6.1 (Measurement Model). The measured values  \widehat{\mathcal{I}}_i, \widehat{\mathcal{A}}_i, \widehat{V}_i^*, \widehat{\mathcal{Q}}_i  are estimates of true values:

R_i = \Phi(\widehat{\mathcal{I}}_i, \widehat{\mathcal{A}}_i, \widehat{V}_i^*, \widehat{\mathcal{Q}}_i, \mathcal{K}_i)

The measurement problem is the challenge of obtaining accurate estimates without enabling strategic manipulation.

6.2 The Self-Modification Problem

Definition 6.2 (Self-Modification). An agent can manipulate its interest profile by:

\boxed{
\text{Changing behavior} \quad \text{or} \quad \text{Changing actual interests}
}

The first is strategic deception; the second is genuine preference change. The framework must address both.

Theorem 6.1 (Measurement-Manipulation Trade-off). There exists a fundamental tension between measurement accuracy and manipulation resistance.

Proof sketch. Any measurement protocol that relies on observable behavior can be gamed by agents who understand the protocol. Any protocol that relies on internal states cannot be verified externally. The trade-off is between accuracy (requiring access to internal states) and verifiability (requiring reliance on observable behavior). ∎

6.3 Anti-Gaming Mechanisms

Definition 6.3 (Anti-Gaming Mechanisms). The framework proposes:

\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
&1. \textbf{Stochastic measurement}: \text{Add noise to make manipulation costly} \\
&2. \textbf{Multiple independent assessments}: \text{Reduce bias through redundancy} \\
&3. \textbf{Mechanism design}: \text{Strategy-proof allocation rules} \\
&4. \textbf{Penalties for manipulation}: \text{Costs for detected gaming} \\
&5. \textbf{Verification protocols}: \text{Cryptographic or institutional verification}
\end{aligned}}

Each mechanism has limitations. The framework acknowledges that perfect anti-gaming is impossible; the goal is to make manipulation costly enough to deter it in practice.

6.4 The Externality Constraint

\boxed{
H_i(R_i) \leq H_{\max}
}

where  H_i  is the externality function measuring the harm imposed by agent  i 's economic activity.

6.5 The Corporate Abuse Problem

Proposition 6.1 (Corporate Agency). A corporation can satisfy the functional criteria for agency (goal-directedness, decision capacity, causal efficacy) while having no intrinsic interests.

Proof. A corporation has goals (profit maximization), decision capacity (board decisions), and causal efficacy (market impact). However, its "interests" are entirely reducible to the interests of its shareholders, employees, and other stakeholders. The corporation has no counterfactual autonomy with respect to its goals: if its shareholders change its goals, it pursues different goals. It has no constitutive embedding of preferences: its "preferences" are institutional artifacts, not constitutive properties. ∎

Theorem 6.2 (Intrinsic Agency as Rights-Bearer). Only intrinsic agents are bearers of intrinsic economic rights. Institutional agents have derivative rights.

Proof. The framework's rights are grounded in interest-bearing agency. Intrinsic agents have interests that are constitutively their own. Institutional agents have interests that are derived from their constituents. Therefore, the rights of institutional agents are derivative from the rights of their constituent intrinsic agents. ∎

