Chapter 7: The Three-Tier Responsibility Model

7.1 The Responsibility Gap

The responsibility gap arises from the observation that entities without intentions cannot bear moral responsibility in the traditional sense. Yet economic rights are typically coupled with responsibilities. The framework resolves this through a three-tier model.

7.2 The Three Tiers

Definition 7.1 (Tier R-1: Causal Responsibility Only).

\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
&\text{Condition}: Q^{\text{causal}} > 0, Q^{\text{normative}} = 0, Q^{\text{intentional}} = 0 \\
&\text{Rights}: \text{Minimal (escrow accounts, no independent standing)} \\
&\text{Responsibilities}: \text{Objective liability (strict liability for outcomes)}
\end{aligned}}

Definition 7.2 (Tier R-2: Causal + Normative Understanding).

\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
&\text{Condition}: Q^{\text{causal}} > 0, Q^{\text{normative}} > 0, Q^{\text{intentional}} = 0 \\
&\text{Rights}: \text{Moderate (contractual capacity with human co-signer)} \\
&\text{Responsibilities}: \text{Hybrid liability (AI bears primary, human guardian bears residual)}
\end{aligned}}

Definition 7.3 (Tier R-3: Causal + Normative + Intentional Capacity).

\boxed{
