Chapter 8: The Interest Attribution Problem

8.1 The Core Question

The Interest Attribution Problem is the challenge of distinguishing architectural goals from owned interests. This is not merely a measurement problem but a conceptual one.

8.2 The Counterfactual Autonomy Criterion

Definition 8.1 (Counterfactual Autonomy). A preference  p  has counterfactual autonomy if:

\boxed{
\text{The system's future policy organization would systematically differ} \\
\text{under satisfaction versus frustration of } p, \text{ independently of} \\
\text{external controller intervention.}
}

This criterion distinguishes between:

· Externally maintained setpoints: The system maintains a goal because it is designed to do so
· Internally maintained preference architectures: The system maintains a preference because its own dynamics preserve it

8.3 The Plasticity Problem

Definition 8.2 (Interest Plasticity). An agent's interests may change over time:

\boxed{
\mathcal{I}_i(t) \neq \mathcal{I}_i(t') \text{ for } t \neq t'
}

This raises the possibility that an agent might "own" its interests at one time but not another, or own some interests but not others. The binary architectural/owned distinction may be too coarse.

Proposition 8.1 (Graded Ownership). Interest ownership is a matter of degree, not kind.

Proof sketch. Consider a spectrum from a thermostat (purely architectural) to a sophisticated AI (fully owned). Intermediate cases exist: a learning thermostat has partially owned interests; a corporation has interests that are partially architectural (encoded in its charter) and partially owned (emergent from its operations). The ownership index  O_i \in [0, 1]  measures the degree of ownership. ∎

Definition 8.3 (Ownership Index). The ownership index  O_i  is:

\boxed{
O_i = g(P_i, C_i, E_i, D_i)
}

where:

·  P_i : Preference persistence
·  C_i : Constitutive coupling
·  E_i : Endogenous maintenance
·  D_i : Depth of embedding

The ownership index allows for partial ownership and resolves some of the hard cases in the Interest Attribution Problem.

