Conclusion

The Universal Theory of Economic Agency has been reconstructed through systematic adversarial destruction and rebuilding. The result is a framework that is mathematically rigorous, philosophically honest, and structurally characterized.

The framework's central contribution is the demonstration that the arrows between ontological, causal, normative, and institutional domains are not logical implications:

\boxed{
\text{What exists} \not\Rightarrow \text{What causes} \not\Rightarrow \text{What deserves} \not\Rightarrow \text{What institutions recognize}
}

Each transition requires an explicit bridge principle. The framework has identified the form of these principles, characterized their logical structure, and formulated the deepest open question:

\boxed{
\textbf{What is the weakest non-arbitrary principle from which positive} \\
\textbf{economic recognition can be derived?}
}

This is not a question the framework can answer alone. It requires philosophical reflection, empirical investigation, and institutional experimentation. But the framework has provided the precise language in which the question can be asked, and the structural characterization that any answer must satisfy.

The question is not whether AI deserves rights. The question is:

\boxed{
\text{Can the same fundamental economic principles apply to any substrate} \\
\text{capable of autonomous value-producing agency?}
}

And if so, what are those principles, and what is their deepest foundation?

The framework does not claim to have answered these questions. It claims to have formulated them with sufficient precision that they can be meaningfully pursued. That, in itself, is a contribution.



This dissertation represents a theoretical research framework. It is not a legal proposal, a policy recommendation, or a definitive philosophical argument. It is an attempt to make precise a question that may be important for the future of civilization: what properties make any intelligence an economically relevant agent, and what rights should follow from those properties? The framework remains open to revision, falsification, and improvement through collaborative critique.


