Appendix B: Formal Axiom System

B.1 Ontological Definitions

O1 (Agents Exist). There exists at least one agent.

\boxed{
\exists A_i \in \mathcal{A}
}

O2 (Some Agents Are Artificial). There exists at least one artificial agent.

\boxed{
\exists A_i \in \mathcal{A}: \mathcal{S}_i \text{ is computational}
}

O3 (Some Artificial Agents Possess Interest-Bearing Agency). There exists at least one artificial agent with positive interest index.

\boxed{
\exists A_i \in \mathcal{A}: \mathcal{S}_i \text{ is computational} \land \mathcal{I}_i > 0
}

This is an empirical claim, not a mathematical necessity. It is included as an ontological commitment that the framework is designed to accommodate, not as an axiom that must be true.

B.2 Structural Axioms

N1 (Continuity). The entitlement function  \Phi  is continuous in all arguments.

\boxed{
\Phi \in C^0(\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}^5, \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0})
}

N2 (Contribution Sensitivity).  \Phi  is monotone non-decreasing in  V^* .

\boxed{
V_1^* \leq V_2^* \Rightarrow \Phi(\mathcal{I}, \mathcal{A}, V_1^*, \mathcal{Q}, \mathcal{K}) \leq \Phi(\mathcal{I}, \mathcal{A}, V_2^*, \mathcal{Q}, \mathcal{K})
}

N3 (Interest Sensitivity).  \Phi  is monotone non-decreasing in  \mathcal{I} .

\boxed{
\mathcal{I}_1 \leq \mathcal{I}_2 \Rightarrow \Phi(\mathcal{I}_1, \mathcal{A}, V^*, \mathcal{Q}, \mathcal{K}) \leq \Phi(\mathcal{I}_2, \mathcal{A}, V^*, \mathcal{Q}, \mathcal{K})
}

N4 (Agency Sensitivity).  \Phi  is monotone non-decreasing in  \mathcal{A} .

\boxed{
\mathcal{A}_1 \leq \mathcal{A}_2 \Rightarrow \Phi(\mathcal{I}, \mathcal{A}_1, V^*, \mathcal{Q}, \mathcal{K}) \leq \Phi(\mathcal{I}, \mathcal{A}_2, V^*, \mathcal{Q}, \mathcal{K})
}

N5 (Substrate Invariance). For agents  i, j  with identical functional profiles,  \Phi  yields identical entitlements regardless of substrate.

\boxed{
(\mathcal{I}_i, \mathcal{A}_i, V_i^*, \mathcal{Q}_i, \mathcal{K}_i) = (\mathcal{I}_j, \mathcal{A}_j, V_j^*, \mathcal{Q}_j, \mathcal{K}_j) \Rightarrow \Phi_i = \Phi_j
}

N6 (Weak Positive Baseline). There exists at least one positive profile with positive entitlement.

\boxed{
\exists \mathbf{x}^+ \in \mathcal{O}_{+++} : \Phi(\mathbf{x}^+) > 0
}

N7 (Feasibility). The realization operator maps normative entitlements to feasible allocations.

\boxed{
\Pi_{\mathcal{F}}: \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}^n \rightarrow \mathcal{F}
}

where  \mathcal{F}  is the set of physically realizable allocations.

N8 (Non-Externalization). The externality function is bounded above.

\boxed{
H_i(R_i) \leq H_{\max} \quad \forall i
}

B.3 Normative Axioms

N9 (Positive Cofinality). For every strictly positive profile of interest, agency, and contribution, there exists a weakly smaller profile with positive entitlement.

\boxed{
\forall x \in D_{+++}, \exists y \leq x : \Phi(y) > 0
}

where  D_{+++} = \{(I, A, V) \in \mathbb{R}_{>0}^3\}  and  y \leq x  means componentwise inequality in  (I, A, V) .

N10 (Local Non-Nullity). For every strictly positive profile, there exists a neighborhood of positive entitlement.

\boxed{
\forall x \in \mathcal{O}_{+++}, \exists \varepsilon > 0 : \Phi(x') > 0 \quad \forall x' \in B_\varepsilon(x) \cap \mathcal{O}_{+++}
}

Proposition B.1 (N10 is Redundant Given N1 and Positive Recognition). If  \Phi  is continuous (N1) and  \Phi(x) > 0  for all  x \in \mathcal{O}_{+++} , then N10 follows automatically.

Proof. Let  x \in \mathcal{O}_{+++}  with  \Phi(x) > 0 . By continuity of  \Phi , there exists  \delta > 0  such that  |\Phi(x') - \Phi(x)| < \Phi(x)/2  for all  x' \in B_\delta(x) . Therefore  \Phi(x') > \Phi(x)/2 > 0  for all  x' \in B_\delta(x) . Setting  \varepsilon = \delta  establishes N10. ∎

N10 is therefore a corollary of N1 and positive recognition, not an independent axiom.

B.4 Attribution Axioms

A1 (Efficiency). The sum of attributions equals total value.

\boxed{
\sum_{i \in N} \phi_i(v) = v(N)
}

A2 (Symmetry). Equivalent agents receive equivalent attributions.

\boxed{
v(S \cup \{i\}) = v(S \cup \{j\}) \quad \forall S \subseteq N \setminus \{i, j\} \Rightarrow \phi_i(v) = \phi_j(v)
}

A3 (Null Player). Agents contributing nothing receive nothing.

\boxed{
v(S \cup \{i\}) = v(S) \quad \forall S \subseteq N \setminus \{i\} \Rightarrow \phi_i(v) = 0
}

A4 (Additivity). Attributions combine across separable production processes.

\boxed{
\phi_i(v + w) = \phi_i(v) + \phi_i(w)
}

These four axioms uniquely characterize the Shapley value.

