Appendix E: Formal Verification of the Saturating Entitlement Function

E.1 Statement

\Phi = \kappa(\mathcal{K}) \cdot \frac{(1 + \mathcal{I}^{\alpha})(1 + \mathcal{A}^{\beta})(1 + (V^*)^{\gamma})(1 + \mathcal{Q}^{\delta}) - 1}{C + (1 + \mathcal{I}^{\alpha})(1 + \mathcal{A}^{\beta})(1 + (V^*)^{\gamma})(1 + \mathcal{Q}^{\delta}) - 1}

with  \alpha, \beta, \gamma, \delta > 0 ,  \kappa(\mathcal{K}) > 0 ,  C > 0 .

E.2 Verification of Axiom N1 (Continuity)

Claim.  \Phi  is continuous in all arguments.

Proof. Define:

P = (1 + \mathcal{I}^{\alpha})(1 + \mathcal{A}^{\beta})(1 + (V^*)^{\gamma})(1 + \mathcal{Q}^{\delta}) - 1

Then:

\Phi = \kappa \cdot \frac{P}{C + P}

The function  P  is a product of continuous functions (for  \alpha, \beta, \gamma, \delta > 0 ), hence continuous. The function  g(P) = \frac{P}{C + P}  is continuous for  P \geq 0  since the denominator is strictly positive ( C + P \geq C > 0 ). The product  \kappa \cdot g(P)  is continuous if  \kappa  is continuous. Therefore  \Phi  is continuous provided  \kappa \in C^0 . ∎

E.3 Verification of Axiom N2 (Contribution Sensitivity)

Claim.  \Phi  is monotone non-decreasing in  V^* .

Proof. For  V_1^* \leq V_2^* , since  \gamma > 0 :

(V_1^*)^{\gamma} \leq (V_2^*)^{\gamma}

Therefore  P(V_1^*) \leq P(V_2^*) . The function  g(P) = \frac{P}{C + P}  is strictly increasing in  P :

\frac{dg}{dP} = \frac{C}{(C + P)^2} > 0

Therefore  \Phi(V_1^*) \leq \Phi(V_2^*) . ∎

E.4 Verification of Axiom N3 (Interest Sensitivity)

Claim.  \Phi  is monotone non-decreasing in  \mathcal{I} .

Proof. Symmetric to E.3. ∎

E.5 Verification of Axiom N4 (Agency Sensitivity)

Claim.  \Phi  is monotone non-decreasing in  \mathcal{A} .

Proof. Symmetric to E.3. ∎

E.6 Verification of Axiom N5 (Substrate Invariance)

Claim.  \Phi  depends only on functional profile, not substrate.

Proof. The function depends only on  (\mathcal{I}, \mathcal{A}, V^*, \mathcal{Q}, \mathcal{K}) , not on  \mathcal{S} . Therefore identical functional profiles yield identical entitlements. ∎

E.7 Verification of Axiom N6 (Weak Positive Baseline)

Claim. There exists at least one positive profile with positive entitlement.

Proof. Choose  \mathcal{I} = \mathcal{A} = V^* = \mathcal{Q} = 1 . Then:

P = (1 + 1^{\alpha})(1 + 1^{\beta})(1 + 1^{\gamma})(1 + 1^{\delta}) - 1 = 2^4 - 1 = 15

\Phi = \kappa \cdot \frac{15}{C + 15} > 0

since  \kappa > 0  and  C > 0 . ∎

E.8 Verification of N9 (Positive Cofinality)

Claim.  \Phi  satisfies Positive Cofinality.

Proof. For any  (\mathcal{I}, \mathcal{A}, V^*) \in \mathbb{R}_{>0}^3 , choose  y = (\mathcal{I}, \mathcal{A}, V^*)  itself. Then:

P = (1 + \mathcal{I}^{\alpha})(1 + \mathcal{A}^{\beta})(1 + (V^*)^{\gamma})(1 + \mathcal{Q}^{\delta}) - 1 > 0

since all factors are strictly greater than 1 (for  \mathcal{I}, \mathcal{A}, V^* > 0  and  \alpha, \beta, \gamma, \delta > 0 ).

Therefore:

\Phi(\mathcal{I}, \mathcal{A}, V^*, \mathcal{Q}, \mathcal{K}) = \kappa \cdot \frac{P}{C + P} > 0

since  \kappa > 0 ,  P > 0 , and  C + P > 0 .

Therefore N9 is satisfied with  y = x . ∎

E.9 Edge Cases

E.9.1 All arguments zero:

P = (1 + 0^{\alpha})(1 + 0^{\beta})(1 + 0^{\gamma})(1 + 0^{\delta}) - 1 = 1 - 1 = 0

\Phi = \kappa \cdot \frac{0}{C + 0} = 0

✓

E.9.2  Q = 0 , others positive:

P = (1 + \mathcal{I}^{\alpha})(1 + \mathcal{A}^{\beta})(1 + (V^*)^{\gamma})(1 + 0^{\delta}) - 1

Since  0^{\delta} = 0  for  \delta > 0 :

P = (1 + \mathcal{I}^{\alpha})(1 + \mathcal{A}^{\beta})(1 + (V^*)^{\gamma}) - 1 > 0

Therefore  \Phi > 0 . ✓

E.9.3 Single argument positive, others zero:

For  \mathcal{I} > 0, \mathcal{A} = V^* = \mathcal{Q} = 0 :

P = (1 + \mathcal{I}^{\alpha})(1 + 0^{\beta})(1 + 0^{\gamma})(1 + 0^{\delta}) - 1 = (1 + \mathcal{I}^{\alpha}) - 1 = \mathcal{I}^{\alpha} > 0

Therefore  \Phi > 0 . ✓

E.9.4 Parameter edge case  \alpha = 0 :

Not permitted (strictly positive parameters required).

E.9.5  V^* = 0 ,  \gamma < 1 :

0^{\gamma} = 0 \text{ for } \gamma > 0

The function value is well-defined. The derivative  \partial \Phi / \partial V^*  diverges at  V^* = 0  for  0 < \gamma < 1 , but the function itself is continuous. The order-theoretic monotonicity (N2) holds despite the derivative divergence. ✓

