Appendix F: The Counterexample in Detail

F.1 The Linear Threshold Counterexample

\Phi(\mathcal{I}, \mathcal{A}, V^*, \mathcal{Q}, \mathcal{K}) = \max\{0, \mathcal{I} + \mathcal{A} + V^* - C\}, \qquad C > 0

F.2 Verification of All Structural Axioms

N1 (Continuity): The function  f(x) = \max\{0, x\}  is continuous, and  g(I, A, V^*) = I + A + V^* - C  is continuous. Composition of continuous functions is continuous. ✓

N2 (Contribution Sensitivity): For fixed  I, A, Q, K , increasing  V^*  increases the argument of  \max\{0, \cdot\} . Since  \max\{0, \cdot\}  is monotone non-decreasing,  \Phi  is non-decreasing in  V^* . ✓

N3 (Interest Sensitivity): Symmetric to N2. ✓

N4 (Agency Sensitivity): Symmetric to N2. ✓

N5 (Substrate Invariance): Depends only on functional profile. ✓

N6 (Weak Positive Baseline): For  I + A + V^* > C ,  \Phi > 0 . ✓

F.3 Zero-Entitlement Region

For any profile with:

0 < \mathcal{I} + \mathcal{A} + V^* \leq C

we have:

\Phi(\mathcal{I}, \mathcal{A}, V^*, \mathcal{Q}, \mathcal{K}) = 0

This is a non-empty region of the positive orthant. For example, with  I = A = V^* = C/4 :

\Phi(C/4, C/4, C/4, Q, K) = \max\{0, C/4 + C/4 + C/4 - C\} = \max\{0, -C/4\} = 0

F.4 The Implication

The counterexample demonstrates that structural axioms alone do not generate universal positive entitlement. The zero-entitlement region corresponds to a "recognition threshold": agents below the threshold receive nothing, regardless of positive interest, agency, and contribution.

This is precisely the kind of institution the framework aims to rule out. The Positive Cofinality Principle (N9) is introduced to exclude threshold-based expropriation.

