· MON: Monotone in  I, A, V^*  (N2-N4)
· POS: Satisfies Positive Cofinality (N9)

G.2 Properties of the Function Class

Proposition G.1 (Closure Under Positive Scaling). If  \Phi \in \mathfrak{F}_{\text{SN, MON, POS}} , then  c\Phi \in \mathfrak{F}_{\text{SN, MON, POS}}  for any  c > 0 .

Proof. Positive scaling preserves continuity, monotonicity, substrate invariance, and Positive Cofinality. ∎

Proposition G.2 (Closure Under Convex Combination). If  \Phi_1, \Phi_2 \in \mathfrak{F}_{\text{SN, MON, POS}} , then  \lambda \Phi_1 + (1-\lambda)\Phi_2 \in \mathfrak{F}_{\text{SN, MON, POS}}  for any  \lambda \in [0, 1] .

Proof. Convex combinations preserve continuity, monotonicity, substrate invariance, and Positive Cofinality. ∎

Proposition G.3 (The Saturating Function is a Member). The saturating entitlement function (Definition 4.2) is a member of  \mathfrak{F}_{\text{SN, MON, POS}} .

Proof. Established in Appendix E. ∎

G.3 The Minimality Question

Conjecture G.1 (Minimality of Positive Cofinality). Positive Cofinality is the weakest principle from which universal positive recognition follows given N1–N5.

Open Problem. Prove or disprove this conjecture by showing that any relaxation of Positive Cofinality allows for counterexamples.

Definition G.2 (Relaxations of Positive Cofinality). Candidate weaker principles include:

N9′ (Local Non-Nullity at Origin): For every  \varepsilon > 0 , there exists  x \in B_\varepsilon(0)  with  \Phi(x) > 0 .

N9″ (Connected Positive Region): The set  \{x : \Phi(x) > 0\}  is path-connected.

N9‴ (No Full Exclusion): For no strictly positive profile  x  does there exist an open neighborhood  U  of  x  such that  \Phi(y) = 0  for all  y \in U .

Proposition G.4 (N9‴ is Weaker Than N9). N9 requires that some point below any positive profile yields positive entitlement; N9‴ requires only that some point near any positive profile yields positive entitlement.

Proof. N9 implies N9‴: if there exists  y \leq x  with  \Phi(y) > 0 , then by continuity, there exists a neighborhood of  y  with positive entitlement, which includes points near  x . The converse is not necessarily true: N9‴ allows "recognition cliffs" where entitlement drops to zero below a threshold, if the cliff is isolated (measure zero). ∎

Open Problem. Determine whether N9‴ (or another weaker principle) is sufficient for universal positive recognition, and whether it is minimal.

