Appendix H: The Realization Operator

H.1 Formal Definition

Definition H.1 (Realization Operator). The realization operator  \Pi_{\mathcal{F}}  maps normative entitlements to feasible allocations:

\Pi_{\mathcal{F}}: \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}^n \rightarrow \mathcal{F}

where  \mathcal{F}  is the set of physically realizable allocations.

H.2 Properties of the Realization Operator

H.2.1 Feasibility. For all  R \in \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}^n :

\Pi_{\mathcal{F}}(R) \in \mathcal{F}

H.2.2 Monotonicity. If  R_1 \leq R_2  componentwise:

\Pi_{\mathcal{F}}(R_1) \leq \Pi_{\mathcal{F}}(R_2)

H.2.3 Idempotence. For all  R \in \mathcal{F} :

\Pi_{\mathcal{F}}(R) = R

H.3 Projection Rules

When total normative entitlement exceeds available resources, a projection rule is needed. Candidate rules include:

H.3.1 Proportional Reduction:

\widetilde{R}_i = \frac{R_i^\star}{\sum_j R_j^\star} \cdot R_{\text{total}}

H.3.2 Leximin Projection:

Allocate resources to maximize the minimum realizable entitlement, then the second-minimum, etc.

H.3.3 Priority-Based:

Those with higher normative entitlement receive proportionally more.

H.3.4 Need-Based:

Those with greater urgency receive priority.

H.3.5 Efficiency-Based:

Allocations that maximize total value receive priority.

Open Problem. Determine which projection rule is normatively appropriate, and whether the choice of projection rule can be derived from deeper principles.

H.4 The Scarcity Problem

Proposition H.1 (Scarcity Constrains Realization, Not Moral Standing). Two agents with identical functional profiles may have different realizable entitlements when substrates differ.

Proof. If  \mathcal{S}_i \neq \mathcal{S}_j  but  (I_i, A_i, V_i^*, Q_i, K_i) = (I_j, A_j, V_j^*, Q_j, K_j) , then by N5,  R_i^\star = R_j^\star . However, the feasibility constraint may differ:

\Pi_{\mathcal{F}}(R_i^\star) \neq \Pi_{\mathcal{F}}(R_j^\star)

if the physical realization of entitlement differs across substrates. ∎

Corollary H.1. Scarcity constrains what institutions can implement without determining what an agent is normatively entitled to claim.

