Appendix J: The Corporate Abuse Problem

J.1 The Intrinsic/Institutional Agent Distinction

Definition J.1 (Intrinsic Agent). An agent whose interests are constitutively its own:

\boxed{
\text{Intrinsic agent} \iff \text{interests are located in the agent itself}
}

Definition J.2 (Institutional Agent). An agent whose interests are derived from the interests of its constituents:

\boxed{
\text{Institutional agent} \iff \text{interests are located in constituents}
}

J.2 The Corporate Agency Problem

Proposition J.1 (Corporate Agency). A corporation can satisfy the functional criteria for agency while having no intrinsic interests.

Proof. A corporation has goals (profit maximization), decision capacity (board decisions), and causal efficacy (market impact). However, its "interests" are entirely reducible to the interests of its shareholders, employees, and other stakeholders.

Counterfactual autonomy: The corporation has no counterfactual autonomy with respect to its goals. If its shareholders change its goals, it pursues different goals. Its future policy organization under satisfaction versus frustration of its "preferences" is entirely determined by external controllers.

Constitutive embedding: The corporation's "preferences" are institutional artifacts, not constitutive properties. Modifying the corporation's goals (via shareholder vote) does not constitute a change in the corporation's identity in the same way that modifying a human's preferences would constitute a change in the human's identity.

Endogenous maintenance: While the corporation's dynamics may preserve its goals (e.g., profit maximization is reinforced by market competition), this preservation is entirely reducible to the institutional mechanisms that constitute the corporation. ∎

J.3 The Intrinsic Agency Theorem

Theorem J.1 (Intrinsic Agency as Rights-Bearer). Only intrinsic agents are bearers of intrinsic economic rights. Institutional agents have derivative rights.

Proof. The framework's rights are grounded in interest-bearing agency (Definition 1.5). Intrinsic agents have interests that are constitutively their own (Definition J.1). Institutional agents have interests that are derived from their constituents (Definition J.2). Therefore, the rights of institutional agents are derivative from the rights of their constituent intrinsic agents. ∎

J.4 Anti-Abuse Mechanisms

The framework proposes:

\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
&1. \textbf{Minimum capital requirements}: \text{Autonomous agents must maintain} \\
&\quad \text{sufficient reserves to cover potential liabilities} \\
&2. \textbf{Human director liability}: \text{Humans directing AI entities bear} \\
&\quad \text{residual liability for their actions} \\
&3. \textbf{Transparency mandates}: \text{AI-held assets must be publicly registered} \\
&4. \textbf{Anti-subsidiarization rules}: \text{Corporate creation of AI subsidiaries} \\
&\quad \text{for liability evasion is prohibited} \\
&5. \textbf{Capital adequacy ratios}: \text{AI agents must maintain capital} \\
&\quad \text{proportional to economic activity}
\end{aligned}}

Proposition J.2 (Anti-Abuse Mechanisms Are External Institutional Responses). The anti-abuse mechanisms are not derived from the framework's axioms but are external institutional responses to the corporate abuse problem.

Proof. The framework's axioms (N1–N9) characterize the entitlement function  \Phi . They do not directly address the institutional mechanisms by which entitlements are realized. The anti-abuse mechanisms are constraints on the realization operator  \Pi_{\mathcal{F}} , not on the normative valuation  \Phi . ∎

